WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Elena compares four optional features on a life policy.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.960
One adds money after a qualifying accidental death.

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.140
Another preserves opportunities to purchase more protection even if health

4
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:15.720
changes. A third adjusts coverage using an inflation measure.

5
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.960
A fourth adds a death benefit linked to premiums under its

6
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.180
formula. These features solve different problems.

7
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.540
The word rider does not mean every feature pays cash immediately

8
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.260
or lasts as long as the base policy.

9
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.260
Identify the covered event, the amount, the election conditions and the

10
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.120
ending provisions. Then read the cost.

11
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.820
A familiar feature name is a starting point, not a substitute

12
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.360
for the actual contract.

13
00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:51.094
Assume a covered two hundred thousand dollar base death benefit and

14
00:00:51.094 --> 00:00:53.674
a fifty thousand dollar accidental-death rider.

15
00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:59.234
If the death meets both provisions and no adjustment applies, the

16
00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:01.694
total is two hundred fifty thousand dollars.

17
00:01:02.034 --> 00:01:04.934
It is not automatically four hundred thousand.

18
00:01:05.954 --> 00:01:09.514
Double indemnity describes a design that adds an amount equal to

19
00:01:09.514 --> 00:01:13.074
the base benefit; other designs add different amounts.

20
00:01:14.014 --> 00:01:18.054
Read whether the accident caused the death, the required time between

21
00:01:18.054 --> 00:01:22.314
injury and death, exclusions and the rider's ending age.

22
00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:26.794
If a death qualifies under the base policy but not the

23
00:01:26.794 --> 00:01:30.774
accident rider, the base benefit may still be payable under its

24
00:01:30.774 --> 00:01:35.356
own terms. A guaranteed-insurability rider allows specified additional

25
00:01:35.356 --> 00:01:38.756
insurance without new evidence of insurability at stated opportunities.

26
00:01:39.976 --> 00:01:43.176
Those may be scheduled ages or qualifying events such as marriage,

27
00:01:43.476 --> 00:01:47.716
birth or adoption. The actual rider determines which opportunities apply.

28
00:01:48.596 --> 00:01:51.676
Suppose Elena's active rider permits a twenty-five thousand dollar increase

29
00:01:51.676 --> 00:01:52.816
at the next option date.

30
00:01:53.496 --> 00:01:57.376
A health change does not automatically remove that protected opportunity, but

31
00:01:57.376 --> 00:02:00.336
she must meet the election requirements and pay the applicable premium.

32
00:02:00.936 --> 00:02:03.616
The right is not unlimited insurance whenever she wants it.

33
00:02:03.996 --> 00:02:07.596
It also differs from guaranteed renewability, which concerns continuing

34
00:02:07.596 --> 00:02:12.516
existing coverage, and conversion, which changes eligible coverage into

35
00:02:12.516 --> 00:02:16.714
another permitted form. A cost-of-living rider can increase life coverage

36
00:02:16.714 --> 00:02:20.174
using a stated inflation measure, often the Consumer Price Index.

37
00:02:20.574 --> 00:02:24.229
Suppose the question explicitly provides a three percent increase on one

38
00:02:24.229 --> 00:02:25.534
hundred thousand dollars of coverage.

39
00:02:26.034 --> 00:02:28.894
Three thousand dollars is the increase, so the new amount is

40
00:02:28.894 --> 00:02:30.154
one hundred three thousand dollars.

41
00:02:30.594 --> 00:02:33.534
This is a coverage calculation, not a three percent cash dividend

42
00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:35.494
or guaranteed investment return.

43
00:02:35.914 --> 00:02:39.834
Check how often adjustments occur, any limits, the effect of declining

44
00:02:39.834 --> 00:02:42.174
an increase and additional insurance costs.

45
00:02:42.574 --> 00:02:46.614
Some designs waive new evidence of insurability for permitted increases.

46
00:02:47.074 --> 00:02:51.474
Do not assume every rider adjusts annually or provides increased protection

47
00:02:51.474 --> 00:02:52.754
at no added cost.

48
00:02:53.389 --> 00:02:57.649
A return-of-premium death benefit rider can add an amount related to

49
00:02:57.649 --> 00:03:00.409
premiums to the death benefit when the insured dies.

50
00:03:01.189 --> 00:03:05.369
Assume the fictional contract provides a two hundred thousand dollar base

51
00:03:05.369 --> 00:03:09.809
benefit plus twelve thousand dollars of eligible accumulated premiums, with

52
00:03:09.809 --> 00:03:11.529
no deductions or other adjustments.

53
00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:14.869
The total is two hundred twelve thousand dollars.

54
00:03:15.369 --> 00:03:19.629
Read the formula because withdrawals and charges can affect the rider

55
00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:24.369
amount. This differs from return-of-premium term insurance that refunds

56
00:03:24.369 --> 00:03:29.569
specified eligible premiums when the insured survives the stated term and

57
00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:30.969
meets the policy conditions.

58
00:03:31.669 --> 00:03:34.829
Ask whether the triggering event is death or survival.

59
00:03:36.109 --> 00:03:38.669
Similar names do not establish identical benefits.

60
00:03:39.415 --> 00:03:43.955
Now Elena asks which feature addresses concern about becoming difficult to

61
00:03:43.955 --> 00:03:48.755
insure later. A defined guaranteed-insurability opportunity directly

62
00:03:48.755 --> 00:03:53.975
addresses that concern. An accidental-death benefit addresses a different

63
00:03:53.975 --> 00:03:59.035
cause-of-death condition. An inflation rider addresses the stated change in

64
00:03:59.035 --> 00:04:03.375
coverage. The final rider adds a death benefit based on eligible

65
00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:04.895
premiums under its formula.

66
00:04:04.895 --> 00:04:09.315
On an exam, underline the event and the requested result.

67
00:04:10.035 --> 00:04:14.315
Then calculate only the amounts that the facts trigger.

68
00:04:15.335 --> 00:04:18.975
Check whether the option must be elected and whether a deadline,

69
00:04:19.395 --> 00:04:22.475
exclusion or coverage limit changes the answer.

70
00:04:23.515 --> 00:04:28.275
That approach connects the rider's purpose to the actual case instead

71
00:04:28.275 --> 00:04:30.855
of choosing the most attractive sounding feature.
